### What was the goal
The goal was to implement a frontend that translates ChocoPy to Bril, utilizing an existing ChocoPy parser and generate correct Bril code for a representative subset of the language. Features that I would cover concerning ChocoPy's language will be:
- Types: Integers, Booleans
- Basic Operations: + - * // % < <= > >= == != and or not
- Local variable assignment and declaration
- If/else and while loops
- Function definition, argument passing, return statements

### What did you do?
I implemented what my goal was, covering almost all of the features I stated concerning ChocoPy’s language, except for strings and characters. I realized that Bril does not support characters or strings, and that implementing these in Bril would be essentially “hardcoded”. I originally thought that regardless of this I could make a string and character table, storing the actual values correlated to an integer id that would be stored in bril. However, I realized attempting to print a string or character would not work as bril would not support printing a type that doesn’t exist in itself. In order to implement strings and characters, I would need to implement several more features in Bril like lists, as well as make an entire wrapper around bril. As a result, I had decided not to implement characters and strings.  Overall, I am very satisfied with both how I implemented my solution and how it works. Funnily enough, for booleans there was also a mismatch because Python has “True” and brili has “true”, which I thought was similar enough to be negligible. Furthermore, I also implemented modulo by making a compile_modulo command that puts sequential operations that function as the modulus operator.
